import{_ as t,o as s,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.ied94ccl.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Debugging a Cell","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"debugger.md","filePath":"debugger.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"debugger.md"};function i(r,e,l,h,d,g){return s(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="Debugging-a-Cell" tabindex="-1">Debugging a Cell <a class="header-anchor" href="#Debugging-a-Cell" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Debugging a Cell {#Debugging-a-Cell}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A code cell can be stepped through line by line, on this machine or on a region&#39;s worker. Click <strong>🐞</strong> in the cell&#39;s header, or pick <strong>Debug the selected cell</strong> in the command palette. What is stepped is the cell&#39;s current text in the editor, even unsaved. Clicking 🐞 again on the same cell ends the session.</p><p>The session opens a workspace over the notebook: the source with the current line marked, a rail with the call stack, breakpoints and watches, the variables, and a scratchpad. <strong>Esc</strong> closes the workspace and leaves the session running, shown as a ribbon under the cell with where it stopped, how many steps it has taken and a <strong>⤢</strong> to reopen it.</p><p><img class="slate-shot" src="https://github.com/kahliburke/KaimonSlate.jl/releases/download/docs-assets/debugger-workspace.png" alt=""></p><h2 id="stepping" tabindex="-1">Stepping <a class="header-anchor" href="#stepping" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stepping&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Button</th><th style="text-align:right;">Key</th><th style="text-align:right;">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">⤷ Next</td><td style="text-align:right;">F10</td><td style="text-align:right;">Runs to the next line in this frame.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">⤓ Into</td><td style="text-align:right;">F11</td><td style="text-align:right;">Steps into a call on this line. With more than one, a list asks which.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">⤒ Out</td><td style="text-align:right;">⇧F11</td><td style="text-align:right;">Finishes this call and stops at the caller. At the cell&#39;s top level, finishes the statement.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">▶▶ Continue</td><td style="text-align:right;">F5</td><td style="text-align:right;">Runs to the next breakpoint, or to the end.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">▶| Skip</td><td style="text-align:right;"></td><td style="text-align:right;">At a breakpoint: disables it and continues.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">■ Stop</td><td style="text-align:right;">⇧F5</td><td style="text-align:right;">Ends the session.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The keys work only while a session is running, so F5 still reloads the page otherwise. They can be rebound in <strong>Keyboard shortcuts</strong>.</p><p>Your own code is interpreted, so it can be stepped: the notebook&#39;s cells and packages you develop from a local checkout. Base, the standard libraries and registered packages run compiled, at full speed, and Into passes over their calls and says so. Turn on <strong>step into library code</strong> to be offered them as well. Picking one interprets that whole module from then on, which is slower; the rail lists what is being interpreted, and <strong>✕</strong> on a module goes back to running it compiled.</p><p>A single step stops after 500 interpreter steps rather than run on indefinitely, and there is no run-to-line: set a breakpoint and continue instead.</p><h2 id="breakpoints" tabindex="-1">Breakpoints <a class="header-anchor" href="#breakpoints" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Breakpoints&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Click in the gutter of a cell&#39;s editor, or press <strong>F9</strong> on a line, to set a breakpoint. They can be set before a session starts, which is the usual order: mark the line, then start. A line in a package works the same way from the workspace&#39;s source view.</p><p>The rail lists every breakpoint. <strong>●</strong>/<strong>○</strong> enables or disables one without clearing it, <strong>when</strong> gives it a condition, and <strong>✕</strong> clears it. A condition is a Julia expression evaluated in the frame each time the line runs; the breakpoint stops only when it is <code>true</code>. A condition that does not parse is refused when you set it. Breakpoints are kept by the hub for as long as it runs. They are not saved in the notebook.</p><h2 id="Looking-around" tabindex="-1">Looking around <a class="header-anchor" href="#Looking-around" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Looking around {#Looking-around}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>The call stack</strong> lists the frames, current first. Click a caller to look at it; <strong>▸ back to current</strong> returns.</p></li><li><p><strong>Variables</strong> shows the frame&#39;s locals and the cell&#39;s bindings, each with its type and a short value. A binding still holding its value from the previous run is dimmed until this run assigns it. Values that changed with the last step flash.</p></li><li><p><strong>The scratchpad</strong> evaluates Julia in the paused frame, with completion that offers the frame&#39;s locals first. ↑ and ↓ recall what you typed before. An evaluation can change state, as it would at a REPL.</p></li></ul><h2 id="watches" tabindex="-1">Watches <a class="header-anchor" href="#watches" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Watches&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <strong>watch</strong> samples an expression every time a line runs, without stopping there. Add one with <strong>+</strong> in the rail&#39;s watches at the current line. Each row shows the latest value; click it to plot every sample so far, which is how a value&#39;s behaviour over a long loop is seen. A watch keeps up to 200,000 samples.</p><p>A <strong>live view</strong> re-evaluates an expression, or renders another cell&#39;s output, after every step, as a tile beside the source.</p><h2 id="Cells-on-a-region" tabindex="-1">Cells on a region <a class="header-anchor" href="#Cells-on-a-region" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cells on a region {#Cells-on-a-region}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A cell tagged for a region is stepped on that region&#39;s worker, and the workspace shows where. A region with no allocation yet waits for one first.</p><h2 id="Sessions-and-agents" tabindex="-1">Sessions and agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#Sessions-and-agents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sessions and agents {#Sessions-and-agents}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>There is one session per notebook. Anyone with the notebook open can step it. Ending or replacing a session someone else started asks them first, with <strong>Allow</strong> or <strong>Keep it</strong>.</p><p>The agent can debug through its <code>dbg_start</code>, <code>dbg_step</code>, <code>dbg_frame</code>, <code>dbg_eval</code>, <code>dbg_break</code> and <code>dbg_watch</code> tools. When <strong>Settings → Specialists → Only the debug specialist may step code</strong> is on, stepping is left to a debugging specialist the agent brings in, and the workspace shows who is driving.</p>',22)])])}const u=t(o,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
